unit-redis-1.0.3 (1) keel; urgency=low

  * bin/redispass.py draws its password box on the terminal.
    firstboot.d/35redispass captures the script's standard output for
    DB_PASS=, and dialog draws on standard output, so an interactive first
    boot would freeze at the Redis password with the box drawn into the
    hook's command substitution, as keel-wordpress 19.0-3 did at the
    MariaDB password on Proxmox (2026-09-30). The answers now go to a copy
    of that pipe and standard output is the terminal. tests/dialog.bats
    runs the script as the hook does, inside a pseudo terminal.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 04:30:00 +0000

unit-redis-1.0.2 (1) keel; urgency=low

  * Declare the administrative account once. 20-keel-acl.conf published it
    as "user admin off", the way keel-mariadb publishes an account with a
    password hash no input produces, and firstboot.d/35redispass declared
    it again with its digest. Redis refuses that outright:

        Error in user declaration 'admin': Duplicate user found.
        A user can only be defined once in config files

    Measured on the booted appliance of the first layer that got that far,
    where it left redis-server.service in a restart loop and the first boot
    hook failing. The build time fragment names no administrative account
    now, and the first boot names it once. An account that is not declared
    cannot authenticate either, which was the property that mattered.

  * Take the packaged log file away again when the check created it. Redis
    opens every value its logfile setting is ever given, so the path in the
    packaged configuration is created even though the check passes
    --logfile pointing somewhere else, and the check runs as root:

        redis-server[524]: *** FATAL CONFIG FILE ERROR (Redis 8.0.2) ***
        redis-server[524]: Can't open the log file: Permission denied

    /var/log/redis/redis-server.log, owned by root, in a directory owned by
    the redis user, which is a service that cannot start on any machine
    built from the layer. Measured on the same appliance. The package ships
    no such file and redis makes its own at the first start, so the check
    removes an empty one. A log file with something in it is left alone.

  * Both were found by booting the layer, which is what the boot test is
    for, and neither could have been found by the build: the build's own
    checks passed, because a build runs everything as root.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 02:30:00 +0000

unit-redis-1.0.1 (1) keel; urgency=low

  * Read an INFO reply the way Redis writes it. Redis speaks CRLF: every
    line of an INFO reply ends "\r\n" and redis-cli prints the reply as it
    came, so "tcp_port:6379" is really "tcp_port:6379\r" and the conf
    script's check, anchored with a dollar, never matched it. The first
    build of this component died there, with a server that was up and
    listening on both families and a message that said no Redis answered.
    Measured in the chroot of that build:

        redis-cli INFO server | grep tcp_port | od -c
        t c p _ p o r t : 6 3 7 9 \r \n

    The reply now goes through tr before it is read, in one function both
    checks call, and the stub in tests/conf.bats answers in CRLF so every
    test in that file covers it.

  * A failure prints the server's own log. The check removes the directory
    it runs the server in, so the log that explains a failure went with it
    and the build that failed could not be diagnosed from its own output.
    The fatal path now prints the last twenty lines first.

  * The message that failure prints says which address reported nothing and
    how long the wait was, as two separate facts. The old one read "no Redis
    answered INFO on [127.0.0.1]:6379 after 30 tries" for a check that had
    made one attempt, because the tries belong to the wait on [::1].

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 01:45:00 +0000

unit-redis-1.0.0 (1) keel; urgency=low

  * The Redis component, as a fab unit (decision 0010): a plan, an overlay
    and a conf script, consumed through unit.d by every recipe that carries
    a Redis server. Redis is the third of the three engines decision 0013
    settled on, and the first one whose component is written rather than
    extracted: turnkeylinux/common has no conf/redis, no overlays/redis and
    no plans/turnkey/redis, so there was nothing in the shared tree to take
    out. The upstream appliance configured the server from its own recipe.

  * The server listens on the loopback of both families, and both are
    literal addresses: "bind ::1 127.0.0.1". Debian ships
    "bind 127.0.0.1 -::1", where the dash makes ::1 optional, so a server
    that cannot bind it starts anyway and answers one family. A name is
    never used: Debian maps ::1 to ip6-localhost and never to localhost,
    which is how the PostgreSQL appliance shipped listening on IPv4 only.

  * The declared secret is the password of an ACL user, not requirepass.
    Redis has no database account, and the vocabulary work of decision 0013
    leaves both open. This component chooses the ACL user for two reasons.
    requirepass is the default user's password, so it also locks INFO, which
    is the one thing `keel inspect` reads to report database.server.role,
    and inspect never reads a secret to get past a refusal: an appliance
    with requirepass set cannot say that it is standalone, and the role is
    the seam the replication modes of decision 0013 are built on. And
    requirepass lives in /etc/redis/redis.conf, which the package ships
    root:root 0644, so the upstream appliance wrote the Redis password into
    a world readable file and shipped turnkey-redis-pw to print it back out;
    an ACL rule takes the SHA-256 of a password instead, and this component
    writes that into a file it installs root:redis 0640.

  * So the default account is left able to run one command, INFO, with no
    key and no channel, and the administrative account is published "off",
    which is this component's version of the unusable account keel-mariadb
    publishes: a layer is published once and reused, so a password chosen at
    build time would be the same password everywhere and a random one would
    make the layer irreproducible. firstboot.d/35redispass turns the account
    on from secrets.db_password of the instance description, restarts the
    server, proves the password by connecting, and proves the other half by
    asking for a key with no credentials and requiring NOPERM.

  * Configuration is added as fragments under /etc/redis/redis.conf.d, with
    one appended line in the packaged conffile including them at the end of
    the file, where Redis's own manual says an include belongs when it is
    meant to override. The 110 kB conffile keeps every other default and
    every comment, and an upgrade has one line to merge.

  * The conf script asserts the behaviour and not the file it wrote
    (docs/traps.md): it starts the server from the real configuration, asks
    INFO on [::1] and on 127.0.0.1 in turn, and asks for a key with no
    credentials and requires the refusal. It writes no snapshot and leaves
    no directory behind.

  * No conf-vars. fab lets a unit name the build time variables its conf
    script reads, and this one reads none: the password comes from the
    instance description at first boot, not from the build.

  * No Webmin module. Debian 13 packages webmin-mysql and webmin-postgresql
    and no equivalent for Redis, so an appliance built on this component
    gets the panel core carries and no Redis page in it.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 22:00:00 +0000
